### Investigation of the bundled JavaScript

I inspected the JavaScript shipped with the VS Code extension, specifically `webview/index.js`.

**The following is reconstructed from the compiled/transpiled production JavaScript bundle. It is not Anthropic's original TypeScript/React source code.** I've combined and simplified the relevant sections for readability, while retaining identifiable function names and behaviour. Some variable names and helper calls below are explanatory pseudocode, not exact extracts.

```javascript
// 1. Request sent to the extension backend
getSubagentTranscript(e, t, n) {
  return this.sendRequest({
    type: "get_subagent_transcript",
    sessionId: e,
    agentId: t,
    ...(n !== void 0 && { after: n }),
  });
}

// 2. Initial loading and subsequent refreshes in GRt
const previousFrames = currentFile?.frames;
const lastFrame = previousFrames?.at(-1);

const after =
  refreshCount > 0 &&
  previousFrames !== undefined &&
  typeof lastFrame?.uuid === "string"
    ? {
        count: previousFrames.length,
        uuid: lastFrame.uuid,
      }
    : undefined;

session.getSubagentTranscript(agent.taskId, after).then(
  ({ frames, from }) => {
    if (currentFile === undefined) {
      // Initial load: process all returned frames
      setFile({
        frames,
        built: _W(frames, record),
        record,
      });
    } else {
      // Subsequent loads: append new frames
      // using the existing incremental builder
      setFile({
        frames: [...currentFile.frames, ...newFrames],
        built: JIe(currentFile.built, newFrames, record),
        record,
      });
    }
  }
);

// 3. Transcript rendering in GRt
const groups = BLe(messages);

groups.map((group) => {
  // Elements are constructed before checking expansion
  const elements = group.messages.map(({ msg, idx }) =>
    renderRow(msg, lookup[idx], options)
  );

  if (group.messages.length <= 1) {
    return elements[0];
  }

  const expanded = expandedGroups.has(groupKey);

  // Illustrative only: the actual return uses bundled React components.
  // The group header is always rendered; children appear when expanded.
  return renderGroupHeaderAndChildren(expanded ? elements : null);
});
```

The initial request passes `undefined` as the cursor, so the webview doesn't specify a page size or history limit. Whether the backend applies its own limit would need to be verified separately.

Subsequent requests use an `after` cursor to retrieve newer frames, so some of the functionality for incremental transcript loading already exists.

The rendering path also processes every message group in `BLe()` and constructs React elements for each group's messages before deciding whether to include the expanded content.

I suspect combining paginated historical loading with message-list virtualization would significantly improve the experience, particularly for subagents that have performed hundreds of tool calls.

### Related issues

- [#97230 — VS Code parses complete transcripts when reopening large sessions](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/97230)
- [#105065 — Extension repeatedly reads full transcript histories](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/105065)
- [#69473 — VS Code unresponsive while resuming large sessions (previously marked fixed)](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/69473)

These are related performance problems, but this report specifically concerns opening and rendering an individual subagent transcript in the VS Code webview.

I'm happy to provide additional performance traces or help investigate the relevant code paths further.

**Environment**
- App: ChatGPT macOS desktop app (Codex-based), version 26.825.51511 (latest)
- OS: macOS (Apple Silicon)
- Account: fengw2002@gmail.com (Plus)

**Description**
After deleting several conversations, their titles remain stuck in the desktop app's sidebar "Recent" list. These ghost entries:

1. Show "Unable to load this ChatGPT conversation" when clicked;
2. Fail with "Chat could not be deleted" when using right-click → Delete chat;
3. Fail with "Conversation could not be archived" when using Archive;
4. Redirect to the chatgpt.com homepage when opened via "Open on chatgpt.com" — the conversation no longer exists server-side (e.g. conversation ID: 6a915541-a670-83ea-a3a1-baea4345992d);
5. Do NOT appear in the web sidebar, so the issue is specific to the desktop app's conversation list index.

**Troubleshooting already attempted (none worked)**
1. Fully quitting and relaunching the app;
2. Deleting local app data and caches (~/Library/Application Support/com.openai.codex, ~/Library/Caches/com.openai.codex) — the list was restored identically after relaunch;
3. Signing out and signing back in — ghost entries persist and delete/archive still fail;
4. Confirmed the app is on the latest version.

**Conclusion**
The conversation objects were deleted server-side, but the desktop app's conversation list index retains stale references. Delete/archive requests fail because the target no longer exists, so the list cannot self-heal. The state is account-bound (server-side), independent of device or local data.

**Request**
Please purge the stale entries from my account's desktop conversation list, or fix the sync bug where deleting a non-existent conversation leaves a permanent sidebar entry.

### Describe the bug

If GPT-6.1 Sol is selected as the model for implementation, either explicitly or by using an Auto mode that happens to pick it, the transition from Plan mode to Implementation mode never occurs and it hangs forever "waiting" on plan mode.

### Affected version

GitHub Copilot CLI 1.0.98.

### Steps to reproduce the behavior

1. Set the default model to GPT-6.1 Sol
2. Ask copilot to make a plan
3. Approve the plan in an external tool if there is one
4. Choose to begin implementation in the Copilot prompt
5. See copilot hang claiming it's still waiting on plan review to complete
6. Ctrl+C to cancel the wait
7. Switch to Interactive mode
8. Instruct copilot to implement the plan
9. See copilot open the plan for review, effectively returning to step 3

Alternatively, with no external review tool:
1. Set the default model to GPT-6.1 Sol
2. Ask copilot to make a plan
3. Choose to being implementation in the Copilot prompt
4. See copilot hang claiming it's still waiting on plan review to complete
5. Ctrl+C to cancel the wait
6. Switch to Interactive mode
7. Instruct copilot to implement the plan
8. See copilot go back to step 2 above

### Expected behavior

Choosing to start implementation from the Copilot prompt should start implementation, not hang indefinitely claiming it's still waiting on Plan review to complete.

From Interactive mode when there's already an existing Plan, saying to start implementation should start implementation not trigger a plan review.

### Additional context

- OS: Ubuntu 24.04

