prs.body:
The desktop terminal's attachment protocol (`terminal/connection.ts`, `terminal/writer.ts`) moves to one explicit state machine in `terminal/connection-machine.ts`. `terminalConnection` keeps its signature and adds `dispose()`. Binary output stays off the transition.

### Bugs

| Sequence | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shell exits → socket fires `error` while closing | "Terminal connection failed" banner and `onFailure` for a clean exit | `exited` ignores socket `error` and `close` |
| `exited` → user types, clicks or resizes before the tab closes | Input and size frames sent to the exited PTY | `exited` sends nothing |
| `replay_complete` before `attached`; input after a failure; a second `attached` | Held input silently dropped; keystrokes kept piling up after failure; role and size re-queued | Each message is gated on the phase; held input exists only before `live` |
| Observer clicks, replay drains before the pane's first measurement | Click lost: no size frame is ever sent | Click waits in the unsized pane and takes control at the first size |
| Input after the replay, before the first measurement | Framed at another client's canonical size, resizing the PTY to it | Waits for this pane's size; `canonical` is gone |
| `input.write` or a writer step throws | `writing` stays `true` and the writer jams: no more output or input | `try`/`finally` in both; the rest of the queue still runs |
| Unmount with a replay queued | Queued steps still ran | `dispose()` clears the writer |

Also fixes the `terminal.spec.ts` "a click into the composer while a new terminal is created keeps the focus there" flake (see below).

### State

```ts
type ConnectionState =
  | { status: "restoring"; attachmentID; desired?; wantsControl; pendingInput }
  | { status: "replaying"; attachmentID; role; replayComplete; desired?; wantsControl; pendingInput }
  | { status: "live"; attachmentID; role; pane: LivePane }
  | { status: "exited" }
  | { status: "failed"; failure: "disconnected" | "failed" | "outdated" }
  | { status: "disposed" }

type LivePane =
  | { sized: false; wantsControl; pendingInput }
  | { sized: true; size }
```

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
  [*] --> restoring
  restoring --> replaying: attached (input protocol 1)
  restoring --> failed: attached (old protocol) / close / error
  replaying --> live: replay.drained after replay_complete
  replaying --> exited: exited
  replaying --> failed: close / error
  live --> exited: exited
  live --> failed: close / error
  restoring --> disposed
  replaying --> disposed
  live --> disposed
  exited --> disposed
  failed --> disposed
```

| Phase | `input` | `interacted` | `size.changed` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `restoring`, `replaying` | held | `wantsControl` | `desired` |
| `live`, unsized | held | `wantsControl` | flush held input, else size frame if controller or `wantsControl` |
| `live`, sized | send at pane size | size frame | size frame if controller |
| `exited`, `failed`, `disposed` | ignored | ignored | ignored |

- Effects: `queue-resize`, `queue-replay-end`, `send`, `fail`, `clear-writer`. An event that changes nothing returns the same state object.
- The driver (`connection.ts`) keeps only the synchronous `writing` guard, which drops the query replies ghostty-web emits during `term.write`. It is a call-stack guard, not a phase.
- `writer.ts` replaces `scheduled` + `writing` with one `status: "idle" | "scheduled" | "writing" | "cleared"`, one pre-bound `done`, and writes a lone chunk without the `splice`/`filter` arrays.

### Terminal creation focus flake

- `POST …/terminal` emits `persistent-pty.added` before it answers. When the SSE event lands after the response, its refresh supersedes the one `new()` awaits, so `new()` resolves while the list still lacks the terminal.
- `create()` then stores the tab as active, but the strip does not list it yet. The cancelled focus request is cleared, and when the newer list arrives the strip selects the tab, `Panel.focus` asks for focus again, and the terminal takes it from the composer.
- Fix: a `terminal.created` event in `terminal/machine.ts` adds the created terminal to a known list at once, so the tab is listed when it is selected.

### Performance

Off-transition output path, one short Bun run (`vps`, 200,000 × 512 B chunks through `terminalConnection`, median of 7):

| Messages | `dev` | This PR |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 chunk per message, `done` at once | 81.3 ms | 51.1 ms |
| 1 chunk per message, `done` a tick later | 106.7 ms | 62.1 ms |
| 64 chunks per message | 28.8 ms | 27.7 ms |

### Product calls and deviations from the design

- Output still writes after `exited`, so the shell's last output is not lost; it stops after a failure or unmount.
- `exited` carries no exit code: nothing reads it.
- `state()` and the unused selectors (`isConnectionLive`, `connectionRole`, `connectionFailure`) are not added: no caller needs them, and the terminal surface work uses only the existing interface.
- `terminal.tsx` (now driven by the pane machine from #61941) only adds `connection.dispose()` to its cleanup.
- A synchronous `done` (unmounted terminal) now continues the queue on the next microtask instead of in the same loop.

### Visual

```svg
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="240" height="72" viewBox="0 0 240 72" fill="none">
  <style>
    .l{fill:currentColor;opacity:.55}
    .x{fill:currentColor;opacity:.85}
    .c{fill:currentColor;opacity:.8}
    .b{stroke:currentColor;opacity:.25}
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion:no-preference){
      .l1{animation:in 4s infinite}
      .l2{animation:in2 4s infinite}
      .ex{animation:in3 4s infinite}
      .c{animation:blink 1s steps(1) infinite}
      .ok{animation:in4 4s infinite}
    }
    @keyframes in{0%{opacity:0}10%,100%{opacity:.55}}
    @keyframes in2{0%,20%{opacity:0}30%,100%{opacity:.55}}
    @keyframes in3{0%,40%{opacity:0}50%,100%{opacity:.85}}
    @keyframes in4{0%,60%{opacity:0}70%,100%{opacity:.6}}
    @keyframes blink{0%{opacity:.8}50%{opacity:0}}
  </style>
  <rect x="8.5" y="6.5" width="223" height="59" class="b"/>
  <rect class="l l1" x="18" y="16" width="96" height="6"/>
  <rect class="l l2" x="18" y="28" width="140" height="6"/>
  <rect class="x ex" x="18" y="40" width="56" height="6"/>
  <rect class="c" x="78" y="40" width="3" height="6"/>
  <g class="ok" opacity=".6">
    <path d="M196 47l5 5 10-11" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
  </g>
</svg>
```

issues.body:
### What happened?

[`_expandSkillCommand`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts#L2258-L2266) splits the skill name at the first space:

```ts
const spaceIndex = text.indexOf(" ");
const skillName = spaceIndex === -1 ? text.slice(7) : text.slice(7, spaceIndex);
```

So `/skill:demo, also do X` looks up a skill named `demo,` and doesn't find it so sends the raw slash command to the model as plain text. Further, skills with `disable-model-invocation: true` never appear in the model's skills list, so it just improvises off the raw command line.

### Steps to reproduce

I don't think a repro is necessary since the code clearly parses by the empty space but here's a snippet anyway:

```bash
node --input-type=module <<'NODE'
import fs from 'node:fs';
import os from 'node:os';
import path from 'node:path';
import { AgentSession } from '@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent';

const dir = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'demo-'));
fs.writeFileSync(path.join(dir, 'SKILL.md'), '---\ndescription: demo\n---\nUse this skill.');
const expand = AgentSession.prototype._expandSkillCommand.bind({
    resourceLoader: { getSkills: () => ({ skills: [{ name: 'demo', filePath: path.join(dir, 'SKILL.md'), baseDir: dir }] }) },
    _extensionRunner: { emitError: () => {} },
});
console.log(expand('/skill:demo hello').startsWith('<skill name="demo"')); // true
console.log(expand('/skill:demo, hello').startsWith('<skill name="demo"')); // false
NODE
```

Prints `true` then `false`

### Expected behavior

`/skill:demo, also do X` should expand the skill and pass ", also do X" as args. Since `validateName` only allows `a-z`, `0-9` and hyphens the name could just stop at the first character outside that set. Happy to implement if approved.

### Version

1.5.0

`Conversation.submit({ type: "input" })` with no `content` is accepted. The input is stored as a user entry with no `content` (`{"role":"user","timestamp":...}`), and the run then settles `unanswered`, reason `faulted`, detail `content is not iterable`. The conversation now holds a malformed entry.

TypeScript rejects the call, but a JS caller, or a cast, gets no error at submit time. I hit it by resubmitting after a run failed on a provider 429, meaning "carry on".

Repro, `@earendil-works/pi-durable` 1.4.0, Node 22, provider `cloudflare-workers-ai` (any provider should do):

```ts
import { BACKGROUND_CONTEXT as context } from "@earendil-works/chord/context";
import { builtinModels } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/providers/all";
import { createRegistry, Harness, MemoryStorage } from "@earendil-works/pi-durable";

const harness = await Harness.open(new MemoryStorage(), { models: builtinModels(), registry: createRegistry() }, context);
const conv = await harness.createConversation({ ownership: { kind: "ownerless" }, agent: { model: { provider: "cloudflare-workers-ai", modelId: "@cf/google/gemma-4-26b-a4b-it" } } }, context);
console.log((await (await conv.submit({ type: "input", content: "Say hi." }, context)).wait(context)).status); // done
const bare = await (await conv.submit({ type: "input" } as never, context)).wait(context);
console.log(bare.status, bare.reason, bare.detail); // unanswered faulted content is not iterable
```

Expected: `submit()` throws a validation error and nothing is stored, or the input is stored with empty content. A related question: what is the supported way to continue a conversation whose last run ended in a provider error, without adding a new user message?

## Three independent bugs in core tools

Found while reviewing `packages/coding-agent/src/core/tools/`. Fix branch: https://github.com/IKEASven69/pi/tree/fix/tool-bytes-limit-truncation

### Bug 1: write.ts reports wrong byte count

`write.ts:222` uses `content.length` (UTF-16 code units) instead of actual UTF-8 bytes:

```typescript
// Before
text: `Successfully wrote ${content.length} bytes to ${path}`
// After
text: `Successfully wrote ${Buffer.byteLength(content, "utf-8")} bytes to ${path}`
```

Writing 1000 Chinese characters reports "1000 bytes" when the actual UTF-8 size is 3000 bytes. This misleads the model about file size, affecting subsequent offset/limit decisions in read calls.

### Bug 2: find.ts false "results limit reached" warning

`find.ts:184` uses `>=` to detect limit overflow:

```typescript
const resultLimitReached = relativized.length >= effectiveLimit;
```

Since fd is called with `--max-results N`, any search returning exactly N results triggers a false truncation warning. The model then wastefully re-runs the same search with `limit=2N`.

Fix: request `N+1` results from fd, then check `> effectiveLimit`. If we got more than N, trim back to N and report truncation; if exactly N, no warning.

### Bug 3: truncateLine splits surrogate pairs (emoji)

`truncate.ts:275` uses `line.slice(0, maxChars)` which cuts by UTF-16 code unit. If the cut point falls inside a surrogate pair (emoji, rare CJK), it orphans the high surrogate, producing U+FFFD in grep output:

```typescript
// Before
return { text: `${line.slice(0, maxChars)}... [truncated]`, wasTruncated: true };
// After: check if cut point is a low surrogate, back up by one
if (cut > 0) {
    const code = line.charCodeAt(cut);
    if (code >= 0xDC00 && code <= 0xDFFF) cut -= 1;
}
```

## Verification

All three are minimal, localized changes with no behavioral side effects beyond the fix itself. Asking for maintainer approval to open a PR per the contribution guidelines.

### What happened?

`tool_search` misses a deferred tool when the matching parameter description is behind a local `$ref`. Inlining the same schema makes the tool discoverable. MCP input normalization preserves these references.

### Steps to reproduce

Give a deferred tool named `mcp__ledger__inspect`, described as `Inspect records`, these parameters:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": { "options": { "$ref": "#/$defs/options" } },
  "$defs": {
    "options": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": { "mode": { "type": "string", "description": "reconciliation" } }
    }
  }
}
```

Call `tool_search` with `{"query":"reconciliation"}`: `No matching tools found.` Replace `properties.options` with the referenced object: the tool loads.

Reproduced by calling the unchanged built-in search tool and MCP normalizer directly, with an in-memory tool registry and no loaded user extensions or model calls. This is a tool-level reproduction, not a `pi -ne` CLI run.

### Expected behavior

Descriptions and property names reachable through local references should be searchable, without indexing unused definitions or looping on recursive references. I can implement the fix and regression tests once approved.

### Version

main at `d8981c7ba00a722c897d7270b7f421418512a784` (1.5.0)

**What happened?**

An overlay does not cover a terminal image. If a transcript row holds a Kitty or iTerm2 image and an overlay sits on top of it, the image stays visible and the overlay simply has no rows there: no panel background, no text, a hole exactly the size of the image.

It is deliberate-looking code in `packages/tui/src/tui.ts`:

```ts
export function compositeTuiLine(baseLine, overlayLine, startCol, overlayWidth, totalWidth) {
  if (isImageLine(baseLine))
    return baseLine;
```

so any image row inside the overlay rectangle is passed through untouched.

**Steps to reproduce**

No terminal needed:

```js
import { compositeTuiLine, encodeKitty, allocateImageId } from "@earendil-works/pi-tui";

const png = "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z8BQDwAEhQGAhKmMIQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==";
const row = encodeKitty(png, { columns: 20, rows: 5, imageId: allocateImageId() }) + " ".repeat(20);
const panel = "OVERLAY PANEL ROW".padEnd(40, " ");

compositeTuiLine(row, panel, 30, 40, 100) === row; // true, overlay dropped
```

In pi: open a session where an image was rendered (read a PNG), then open any overlay that covers it (`ui.custom(..., { overlay: true })`). The image is drawn over the panel.

**Expected behavior**

The overlay wins inside its own rectangle. Either delete the Kitty placements that intersect the overlay bounds and re-emit the image when the overlay closes, or at minimum let the panel's lines replace the covered rows.

**Why it matters**

A bordered panel with an opaque background is the normal way to show a picker or dialog. With an image above it, the panel gets a translucent-looking hole and the user reads the image through the UI. Sizing or background changes in the extension cannot fix it, since the row never reaches compositing.

**Version**

@earendil-works/pi-tui 1.4.0 (kitty and iTerm2 protocols; `compositeTuiLine` behaves the same for both).

I can implement this if it is wanted: track image placements per row, and on overlay render/close delete and re-emit the intersecting ones.

### What do you want to change?

Allow pi-env `Connection` to use application-provided adapters that return byte streams instead of always spawning a Node process.

### Why?

Sometimes neither process spawning nor ssh is necessarily available for sandbox tool execution. I have a couple of Pi Durable implementations running as Cloudflare Durable Objects, with tools executing in containers. Cloudflare exposes container execution api through `stdin`/`stdout`/`stderr` Web Streams, not a Node child process.
I wanted to reuse pi-env `RemoteExecutionEnv` and the existing daemon protocol rather than maintain a separate execution implementation.

### How? (optional)

Unsure whether this would be a final pi-env interface, but I got a split along these lines running in a couple of production(ish) deployments. 

It boils down to:
- Separate the protocol client from the Node process adapter from `packages/env/src/connection.ts` into `TransportAdapter` and `ByteTransport`.
- Have one `Connection` protocol accept a factory that receives the synchronization token and a cancellation signal, returning streams and a cleanup callback.
- A shared import that does not pull in Node process/filesystem dependencies.

This would then enable adapters like `localTransport`, `sshTransport` and custom `cloudFlareTransport` or whatever is needed.

```ts
interface ByteTransport {
  stdin: WritableStream<Uint8Array>;   // Send bytes to the daemon
  stdout: ReadableStream<Uint8Array>;  // Receive bytes from the daemon
  stderr?: ReadableStream<Uint8Array>; // Read diagnostic output
  closed?: Promise<void>;             // Observe transport termination
  close(): void | Promise<void>;      // Release the transport
}

type TransportAdapter = (
  options: { token: string; signal: AbortSignal },
) => ByteTransport | Promise<ByteTransport>;

interface ConnectionOptions {
  transport: TransportAdapter;
  onLog?: (text: string) => void;
}

class Connection {
  constructor(options: ConnectionOptions);

  info(): Promise<RemoteInfo>;
  session(): Promise<number>;
  request(
    op: string,
    json: Json,
    options?: RequestOptions,
  ): Promise<Reply>;
  kill(requestId: number, sessionId: number): void;

  close(): void; // Current behavior; awaitable cleanup is a separate decision.
}
```

Would you want pi-env to have generic transport interface like this? Happy to workout what that might be with some feedback or contribute my slop entrypoint split and a remote Cloudflare example if you'll take it.

