Problem: AGENTS.md told agents not to preserve backward compatibility. 1.5.0 (a minor release) removed the `Type` export from `@earendil-works/pi-ai`, which broke `@flue/runtime@2.3.0` and its users.

Change: public APIs (exports, types, CLI flags, file formats) stay compatible outside majors. Agents keep the old form working next to the new one and tag it:

```ts
// remove-in-next-major: Type re-export kept for 1.x consumers
/** @deprecated Use X instead. */
export { Type } from "./legacy.js";
```

Periodic major releases delete everything found by `rg remove-in-next-major`.

cc @badlogic @davidbrai



Release skill (`.pi/skills/release.md`): previously said "`minor` = breaking changes. No major releases.", which contradicts the rule above. Now `minor` = larger non-breaking changes, `major` = breaking changes (`npm run release:major` already exists), and a major release should consider removing `remove-in-next-major` code.

issues.body:
### What happened?

The persisted artifact-library reader rejects an entire valid session as soon as the JSONL transcript exceeds **64 MiB (67,108,864 bytes)**, even when its persisted artifact metadata is tiny. Long-running sessions therefore lose artifact-library discovery/search instead of producing their real artifacts.

This report concerns **read-only artifact extraction**, not a claim that every resume/transcript API has the same limit.

The hard rejection is present in current public main at [`92bed7eeae9eae77fe2bf2a0249bda705d7379d1`, `SessionService.readArtifactLibraryState`](https://github.com/QwenLM/qwen-code/blob/92bed7eeae9eae77fe2bf2a0249bda705d7379d1/packages/core/src/services/sessionService.ts#L3094-L3100). [`readSessionArtifactSnapshot`](https://github.com/QwenLM/qwen-code/blob/92bed7eeae9eae77fe2bf2a0249bda705d7379d1/packages/core/src/services/sessionService.ts#L3192-L3194) calls that reader. The size check happens before artifact events are inspected.

A synthetic valid record of 68,177,881 bytes produced:

```text
SessionTranscriptTooLargeError:
Transcript snapshot for session <synthetic-session-id> is too large to index
(68177881 bytes, max 67108864 bytes)
```

### What did you expect to happen?

Return the small persisted artifact snapshot even when unrelated conversation text makes the transcript large. Large records may take longer to scan, but should be processed lazily or after ordinary records so they do not delay the first ordinary artifact results.

This should keep explicit memory/metadata budgets, cancellation, project ownership, branching/tombstone semantics, and existing mutation safety limits. Simply removing the check and buffering the whole transcript would not address the memory concern.

### Reproduction

Add the following self-contained test as `packages/core/src/services/session-artifact-library-large-repro.test.ts`. It writes only synthetic temporary data: 65 one-MiB user records, one small persisted file-artifact event, and an assistant record. The fixture is removed after the test.

From the repository root after installing the normal development dependencies:

```bash
npm run test --workspace @qwen-code/qwen-code-core -- src/services/session-artifact-library-large-repro.test.ts
```

The `resolves` assertion should return `report.md`; the current reader rejects with `SessionTranscriptTooLargeError` instead.

<details>
<summary>Minimal API-level regression test</summary>

```ts
import { afterEach, describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';
import { promises as fs } from 'node:fs';
import os from 'node:os';
import path from 'node:path';
import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';
import { Storage } from '../config/storage.js';
import { SessionService } from './sessionService.js';
import {
  SESSION_ARTIFACT_PERSISTENCE_VERSION,
  stableSessionArtifactId,
} from './session-artifact-persistence.js';

const temporaryDirectories: string[] = [];

afterEach(async () => {
  await Promise.all(
    temporaryDirectories
      .splice(0)
      .map((directory) => fs.rm(directory, { recursive: true, force: true })),
  );
});

describe('large persisted artifact library', () => {
  it('returns artifacts from a valid transcript larger than 64 MiB', async () => {
    const directory = await fs.mkdtemp(
      path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'qwen-large-artifact-'),
    );
    temporaryDirectories.push(directory);
    const cwd = path.join(directory, 'workspace');
    const runtimeBaseDir = path.join(directory, 'runtime');
    await fs.mkdir(cwd);
    await fs.writeFile(path.join(cwd, 'report.md'), '# Large task report\n');
    const id = randomUUID();
    const artifactId = stableSessionArtifactId(id, 'workspace:report.md');
    const storage = new Storage(cwd, runtimeBaseDir);
    const chats = path.join(storage.getProjectDir(), 'chats');
    await fs.mkdir(chats, { recursive: true });
    const file = path.join(chats, `${id}.jsonl`);
    const common = {
      sessionId: id,
      cwd,
      timestamp: '2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z',
      version: '0.26.4',
    };
    const handle = await fs.open(file, 'w');
    let parentUuid: string | null = null;
    try {
      for (let index = 0; index < 65; index++) {
        const uuid = `user-${index}`;
        await handle.writeFile(
          JSON.stringify({
            ...common,
            uuid,
            parentUuid,
            type: 'user',
            message: { role: 'user', content: 'x'.repeat(1024 * 1024) },
          }) + '\n',
        );
        parentUuid = uuid;
      }
      await handle.writeFile(
        JSON.stringify({
          ...common,
          uuid: 'artifact',
          parentUuid,
          type: 'system',
          subtype: 'session_artifact_event',
          systemPayload: {
            v: SESSION_ARTIFACT_PERSISTENCE_VERSION,
            sessionId: id,
            sequence: 1,
            recordedAt: common.timestamp,
            changes: [
              {
                action: 'created',
                artifactId,
                artifact: {
                  id: artifactId,
                  title: 'Large task report',
                  kind: 'file',
                  storage: 'workspace',
                  workspacePath: 'report.md',
                  source: 'tool',
                  status: 'available',
                  retention: 'restorable',
                  clientRetained: false,
                  createdAt: common.timestamp,
                  updatedAt: common.timestamp,
                },
              },
            ],
          },
        }) + '\n',
      );
      await handle.writeFile(
        JSON.stringify({
          ...common,
          uuid: 'assistant',
          parentUuid,
          type: 'assistant',
          message: { role: 'assistant', content: 'Done' },
        }) + '\n',
      );
    } finally {
      await handle.close();
    }
    expect((await fs.stat(file)).size).toBeGreaterThan(64 * 1024 * 1024);
    const service = new SessionService(cwd, { runtimeBaseDir });
    await expect(
      service.readSessionArtifactSnapshot(id),
    ).resolves.toMatchObject({
      artifacts: [{ id: artifactId, workspacePath: 'report.md' }],
    });
  }, 30_000);
});
```

</details>

### Client information

- macOS, Node.js 22.
- API-level synthetic reproduction against the unmodified 64-MiB reader; the same rejection is independently source-verified in the public main commit linked above.
- This does not require an interactive `/about` session, model request, or provider credentials.

### Login information

Not applicable: the reproduction reads local synthetic session records only.

### Anything else we need to know?

The total transcript size is not a useful bound for the much smaller persisted artifact metadata. A bounded streaming projection plus deferred scheduling is one possible approach; preserving the resource limits is part of the expected behavior. No customer transcripts or private environment details are included.

### Related reports

I searched for the exact error and artifact-library limits before filing. This is distinct from [#13113](https://github.com/QwenLM/qwen-code/issues/13113) (the general 256-MiB transcript-index limit) and [#11493](https://github.com/QwenLM/qwen-code/issues/11493) (64-MiB index-cache admission/repeated scanning). Here, read-only artifact extraction rejects the session at the earlier 64-MiB total-file check.

### Affected version(s)

OpenClaw 2026.9.5 (ec9c1a1) — introduced with the 2026.9.x atomic-move media save path. Confirmed still present because the media save path in this build calls fs-safe `renameNoReplace`.

### Environment

- Deployment: OpenClaw gateway (Telegram channel) in k3s, single replica, state on a **Longhorn RWX PVC** (NFS share-manager, `longhorn-rwx` storage class)
- Host: Ubuntu 22.04, kernel 6.8
- Node runtime: Node 24 (gateway image)
- Filesystem behavior: `renameat2(..., RENAME_NOREPLACE)` returns **EINVAL** on NFS (all operations that use it)

### Description

Inbound Telegram media (documents, photos) silently fails to persist on NFS-backed state volumes. The user-visible effect is the canned reply "⚠️ Failed to download media. Please try again." with no error trace anywhere in `kubectl logs` output from the pod (the pino warn sink that handles this path is not visible in container stdout in this deployment mode, so operators get zero signal).

On our deployment this presented as **five weeks of silently-dead inbound media** (2026-09-29 → 2026-11-05) while text messages worked fine — every `document`/`photo` update failed at the media-save step just before the agent turn was even created.

### Root cause (proven)

The media pipeline calls `saveOkMediaResponse` → fs-safe native `renameNoReplace`, which uses Linux `renameat2` with the `RENAME_NOREPLACE` flag. **NFS does not support `RENAME_NOREPLACE`** and returns `EINVAL`. Every inbound media save therefore fails with "media unavailable: download failed" — not because the download failed, but because the atomic no-replace move is unsupported on the filesystem.

Repro on our side: harness import of `/app/dist/fetch-MjFETjeI.mjs` exported export `i` (`saveRemoteMedia`) surfaces the raw EINVAL immediately after any document arrives in a chat. Sending any document to the bot on a Longhorn RWX (NFS) storage class reproduces it deterministically.

### Local fix (verified working, in production since 2026-11-05 03:12Z)

A NODE_OPTIONS preload shim wrapping the native addon load and patching `renameNoReplace` with a **link()+unlink() fallback** when the native call fails with `EINVAL`/`ENOSYS`/`ENOTSUP`/`EOPNOTSUPP`:

```js
// fs-safe NFS shim: fallback for renameNoReplace on filesystems without renameat2(RENAME_NOREPLACE) support (NFS).
'use strict';
try {
  const realDlopen = process.dlopen.bind(process);
  process.dlopen = function patchedDlopen(module, filename, ...rest) {
    const result = realDlopen(module, filename, ...rest);
    try {
      const b = module.exports || {};
      if (typeof b.renameNoReplace === 'function' && !b.__nfsNoReplaceShim) {
        const real = b.renameNoReplace.bind(b);
        const isUnsupported = (err) => {
          const code = err && (err.code || err.errno);
          if (code === 'EINVAL' || code === 'ENOSYS' || code === 'ENOTSUP' || code === 'EOPNOTSUPP') return true;
          const msg = String((err && err.message) || err);
          return /unavailable on this filesystem|Invalid argument|not supported/i.test(msg);
        };
        b.renameNoReplace = function renameNoReplaceWithNfsFallback(dirFd, from, dirFd2, to) {
          try {
            return real(dirFd, from, dirFd2, to);
          } catch (err) {
            if (!isUnsupported(err)) throw err;
            const fs = require('node:fs');
            const path = require('node:path');
            const dirPath = fs.readlinkSync('/proc/self/fd/' + String(dirFd));
            const dirPath2 = fs.readlinkSync('/proc/self/fd/' + String(dirFd2));
            const src = path.join(dirPath, from);
            const dst = path.join(dirPath2, to);
            fs.linkSync(src, dst); // atomic no-replace: EEXIST if dst exists
            fs.unlinkSync(src);   // publish done; drop the temp name
            return;
          }
        };
        b.__nfsNoReplaceShim = true;
      }
    } catch { /* never break addon loading */ }
    return result;
  };
} catch { /* dlopen not patchable in this runtime — no-op */ }
```

Armed via the deployment env (`NODE_OPTIONS=--dns-result-order=ipv4first --require /data/state/patches/fs-safe-nfs-shim.cjs`). Semantics: `link()` is itself atomic no-replace on NFS and fails with `EEXIST` if the temp target already exists, so the no-replace contract is preserved — we never clobber an existing file, we just publish it a different way.

**Result:** after arming, the first inbound media file in 35 days landed on our deployment (2026-11-05 03:12Z), and three more (photos) at 04:20Z in the same hour. Media ingest is fully restored with this shim in place.

### Upstream ask (two items)

1. **Ship a filesystem-aware fallback inside fs-safe** for `renameNoReplace` when the filesystem doesn't support `RENAME_NOREPLACE` (`EINVAL`/`ENOSYS`/`ENOTSUP`/`EOPNOTSUPP` from `renameat2`) — `link()+unlink()` (atomic no-replace, `EEXIST`-preserving) is a correct, minimal replacement; a "copy to temp + unlink" variant also works if a link is unavailable. This makes OpenClaw usable on NFS-backed storage (Longhorn RWX, EFS, many ONTAP/Gluster setups) without a preload shim.
2. **Surface media-save failures as visible WARN/error lines in pod stdout** — five weeks of "media unavailable: download failed" produced zero operator-visible error output in our deployment. The current pino warn path for this specific failure class isn't reaching container stdout in k8s deployments, which turns a 1-minute diagnosis into a multi-week mystery. A structured stderr WARN with the errno and path would have made this a one-look fix.

### Why we think this matters

NFS-backed state is common in home-lab and small k3s clusters (Longhorn RWX default). Every OpenClaw install on such storage currently loses inbound media silently, with the only signal being a canned user-facing reply. The fix is small, contained to one code path, and we have a working, production-validated patch shape ready.

— filed from the affected deployment (Alessandro's homelab, Telegram bot `@ChiefAlessandroBot`)

