Related: #188422. Complements #190999, which addresses completion-delivery timing.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes Codex follow-up requests failing with “claimed by active work; stop it first” when a tool/configuration change requires replacing an idle parent thread still retained by its native children.

## User Impact

Follow-ups can wait for those children within the existing startup deadline. Stop/reset cancels the wait; foreground claims and additional subscription holds still refuse replacement. No configuration changes or migration are needed.

## Why This Change Was Made

Only the monitor's single background hold qualifies. Preparation releases both the binding lease and native mutation queue before waiting, then reacquires and validates ownership. Children can therefore persist their next model assignment while the parent waits. Prewrite cancellation preserves the healthy shared client; postwrite cleanup cannot enter this retry.

The existing retention map remains authoritative. Ownership notification/expiry selection and child predicates move to their existing owners to keep the capped runtime/monitor modules from growing.

## Evidence

- 160 focused tests pass across lifecycle bindings, startup, warm cleanup, client ownership, parent retention, and configured MCP ownership. The new matrix covers completion, cancellation, a competing foreground claim, and an additional protection; it uses a real protocol-client fixture and the production assignment store/binding lease. Combined local suite time: 249.65s including compilation; CI timing pending.
- Source and targeted test typechecks, type-aware Oxlint for every changed TypeScript file, formatting, line-cap and test-line ratchets pass. Canonical selected SDK preparation emitted 12,771 declaration files with frozen upstream dependencies; no lint rules were weakened.
- Shared autoreview, Codex engine, P0–P2: scoped-clean for the complete staged snapshot against base `05a73c2679f5c7bb660e5f391ee89dd5e618f565`; reviewed tree `940e8c788457c88499e2ecd113a7b9e9f3ef50fa` matches this commit. No remaining actionable findings.
- The 207-line allocation covers the distinct concurrency/cancellation regressions; the shared test-line baseline is unchanged. Against the base: production +145/−41 lines; tests +212/−5 lines.

Baseline control: with only the final regression test copied onto unchanged base `05a73c2679f5c7bb660e5f391ee89dd5e618f565`, completion/cancellation/foreground cases fail because the ownership wait never starts (0 abort listeners instead of 2); the additional-protection refusal control passes. The same four cases pass with this fix. Baseline run: 198.88s including cold compilation.

### Real Gateway/native Codex behavior proof

Ran a one-off live witness on 2026-11-03 against the same production commit `7687ec43f174a43aed03239428b0aad073e673d1`: normal compiled Gateway (`dist/index.js`, `OPENCLAW_TEST_MINIMAL_GATEWAY=0`), bundled native Codex CLI **0.164.0**, existing native Codex login, isolated state and an owned loopback listener. This uses real model calls and native children; there is no synthetic app-server.

