## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: the macOS node runs `camera.snap`, `camera.clip`, `screen.record`, `location.get` and `canvas.present` with default settings and reports success when any supplied param has the wrong type.

## User Impact

A direct `node.invoke` or `openclaw nodes invoke` caller that sends a mistyped field now gets `INVALID_REQUEST` instead of a capture it did not ask for. A `camera.clip` request that opted out of audio can no longer fall back to recording the microphone. Requests with valid or omitted params behave as before.

## Why This Change Was Made

The handlers in `MacNodeRuntime` used `(try? decodeParams(...)) ?? Params()`, so a decode failure became an all-defaults request. `screen.snapshot`, `computer.act` and the PTZ commands already reject undecodable params. This change applies the same contract in the same owner: a small `decodeOptionalParams` turns omitted params into defaults and a supplied-but-undecodable payload into `INVALID_REQUEST`. Camera handlers now decode once, before the permission check, so the `includeAudio` value used to choose the required permissions is the same value passed to `cameraCapture.clip`.

<details>
<summary>Root cause and provenance</summary>

- `MacNodeRuntime.swift` on main: `handleCameraInvoke` decoded clip params twice with `try?` (permission selection at :460-461 and capture at :495-496). Both fell back to `includeAudio ?? true`. Snap (:473), location (:550), screen.record (:656) and canvas.present (:403) had the same fallback.
- `git log -S` traces the fallback to the project rename commit (9a7160786a7), so it was inherited rather than a deliberate leniency. #68181 (5f89cabeb55) already replaced it for `screen.snapshot`. That fix's tests (`rejects malformed params before capture`, `keeps nil params as defaults`) define the contract followed here.
- Shipped senders already send well-typed values. The agent `nodes` tool (`src/agents/tools/nodes-tool-media.ts`, `nodes-tool.ts`) and the `openclaw nodes camera|screen|location` CLI validate before sending, so only raw `node.invoke` callers with bad types see the new error.
- Accepted edge: a raw caller sending `params: null` (serialized as `"null"`) now gets `INVALID_REQUEST`. Main treated it as defaults. No shipped caller sends this. Also, malformed `camera.snap`/`camera.clip` params with camera disabled in OpenClaw now return `INVALID_REQUEST` instead of the disabled-permission error, because params are validated before the permission check.
- Not changed here: the iOS node has the same `try?` fallback (`apps/ios/Sources/Model/NodeAppModel.swift` around :2263-2420). The Linux node plugin checks fields one by one (`extensions/linux-node/src/commands.ts:392` treats a non-boolean `includeAudio` as `true`). Both are separate owners and can't be proven on this Mac, so they are left for follow-ups.

</details>

## Evidence

I packaged ad-hoc arm64 builds of current main (fbbcf0c0cf5) and of this branch with `scripts/package-mac-app.sh`. One isolated loopback Gateway, started from the main build's bundled runtime with a private HOME, state dir and config and the five commands in `gateway.nodes.commands.allow`, served both apps in turn; the change is Swift-only. Each app ran under the same isolated named profile, connected with `configure-remote --direct-url`. Camera was enabled in OpenClaw, and macOS camera, microphone, screen and location permissions were left not-determined. I sent identical `openclaw nodes invoke` requests to both builds:

| Request | main | this branch |
