Closes #182946

## What Problem This Solves

Voice Call cannot currently keep agent consultation available while making the delegated run's tool surface fail closed.

## User Impact

Operators can select a tool-free consultation mode for informational or policy-bounded realtime voice workflows. Existing `safe-read-only`, `owner`, and `none` behavior and defaults are unchanged.

## Why This Change Was Made

This adds explicit `realtime.toolPolicy: "consult-no-tools"` across the shared policy type and Voice Call manifest. In this mode:

- the provider can call `openclaw_agent_consult`;
- configured/custom realtime function tools are omitted;
- the existing `openclaw_end_call` lifecycle control remains available on function-tool providers; native-delegation providers reject this mode because hang-up requires their call-scoped delegated tool;
- the embedded agent receives `toolsAllow: []` and `disableTools: true`;
- routed agent workspace, sandbox session identity, and stricter authority overlays remain intact.

This is enforced during capability construction rather than relying on prompt instructions or a dummy tool.

## Evidence

Validated against `main` at `541a09d30` with Node 24.22.1:

- focused consult and Voice Call suites: 4 files, 123 tests passed;
- consultation, native-policy, tool-construction, and tool-surface guards: 7 files, 187 tests passed; shipped Voice Call consultation-owner proof covers initial and reused sessions through the real agent harness: custom realtime functions are suppressed, end-call remains exposed, delegated create/resume receive zero tools, attempted writes produce no tool result, and no filesystem effect occurs (3 files, 44 tests passed); extension/core test-import boundary check passed;
- core and extensions production typechecks passed;
- changed-file `oxfmt` passed;
- changed-file `oxlint`: 0 warnings, 0 errors;
- docs formatting passed (1,384 files checked); generated config documentation baseline refreshed and verified;
- staged diff privacy/secret scan found no private paths, credentials, or environment-specific material.

Remote CI is pending. No live provider or telephone test was performed. I reviewed the capability flow and understand the fail-closed behavior. The repository's requested `autoreview` skill was not available in this environment; an independent read-only review found no blocking issue or capability broadening.

Closes #180755. Related: #157466.

## What Problem This Solves

Operators with large model catalogues need a personal shortlist they can curate directly while choosing a model.

## User Impact

User impact: click the inline heart beside a model to add or remove a favourite without selecting the model or closing the picker. Favourites appear first and remain available in their provider groups. The Favourites heading uses the same heart icon; there is no separate Manage favourites editor.

Favourites retain this PR's personal, per-signed-in-profile and per-agent scope, persist across reloads and browsers through existing user preferences, and do not change model availability or selection policy. Shared-agent curation and hiding models remain outside this PR.

## Why This Change Was Made

This updates the existing contribution rather than adding a competing implementation. It retains conditional user-preference writes and canonical model/runtime identities, while separating presentation keys so a favourite can safely appear in multiple groups. Inline hearts have accessible pressed state and do not trigger model selection. Keyboard navigation, large-catalogue virtualization and provider settings remain owned by the picker.

Original implementation by @Alix-007. Inline-heart interaction, duplicate provider listing and consistent heart icons requested by @jacobtomlinson; follow-up implementation by @roboclaw-bot. Original contributor commits and history are preserved.

